<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa María (ship)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Santa María was the largest of the three ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s first voyage in 1492 and served as his flagship. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrack">carrack</a> owned by Juan de la Cosa, it ran aground off the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 1492. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> were used to build the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, where Columbus left some 39 men.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-maria-ship.jpg" alt="Santa María (ship)" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Possibly by Edward H. Hart. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolumbus-Santa-Maria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>La Santa María de la Inmaculada Concepción was originally called La Gallega, the Galician. She was built in Pontevedra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, as a small commercial nau or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrack">carrack</a> with a single deck about 18.9 metres long and three small masts. According to Juan Escalante de Mendoza in 1575 she was very little larger than 100 toneladas. Her master and owner was Juan de la Cosa.</p><p>The other ships of the expedition were the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">caravel</a>-type Santa Clara, nicknamed La Niña, and La Pinta. All three had previous owners and were merchant vessels, not made for exploration. Their exact measurements have not survived, and estimates rest on crew anecdotes and on wrecks of similar Spanish and Portuguese ships. Santa María was the slowest of the three but performed well on the Atlantic crossing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Contrary to common belief, the crew was not mainly criminals. Many were experienced seamen from Palos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. The Spanish rulers offered amnesty to convicts who enlisted, but only four accepted. The voyage was financed principally by a syndicate of seven Genovese bankers living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, not, as legend has it, by the queen's necklace given as collateral.</p><p>The crew list names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> as captain-general, Juan de la Cosa as owner and master, Pedro Alonso Niño as pilot, Diego de Arana as master-at-arms and Luis de Torres as interpreter, among others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>On the night of 24-25 December 1492 Santa María ran aground off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>. Researchers disagree whether she struck a coral reef or a sandbar; the Diario's account of a gentle grounding favours a sandbank inside the reef. The Diario records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> ordered Juan de la Cosa to kedge her off with the stern anchor, but de la Cosa rowed to La Niña for help. Columbus then had the mainmast cut, and seams opened.</p><p>Judging the ship beyond repair, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> had her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> stripped and used to build a fort near the modern town of Limonade, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> because the wreck occurred on Christmas Day. One of her anchors is in the Musée du Panthéon National Haïtien in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>. In 2014 Barry Clifford claimed to have found the wreck, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s experts concluded it could not be Santa María, dating its fastenings to the 17th or 18th century. Since no documentation or illustration of the original survives, the many replicas, among them an 1892 Spanish one shown at the Chicago World's Fair of 1893, vary in dimensions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santa María (ship)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Santa María</b>, the flagship of Christopher Columbus's first voyage, ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Its cacique Guacanagarí helped the Spanish salvage the wreck of the <b>Santa María</b> on Christmas Day 1492, and Columbus built the fort of La Navidad with its timbers in his territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-marien">Cacicazgo of Marién</a></li></ul></section>
